            "content": "## Summary\n\nOwner-authored personalization: a `preferredName`, an `about`, and `responsePreferences`, stored per user and rendered into that owner's system prompt on every run. Two toggles gate it — `enabled` (default **on**, because it gates only what the owner typed) and `shareAccountIdentity` (default **off**, because it gates account-derived identity, and defaulting it on would move every existing user's address to the operator's configured provider retroactively).\n\nShips alongside the Handlebars prompt-templating prerequisite that was merged earlier; this branch is the personalization slice built on top of it.\n\n## The structural change underneath\n\nDelivering this required inverting **when prompts render**. `config-loader` used to render each template to a string at boot and the catalog carried only that text, so no template survived to render an owner into. `createModelPromptLoader().resolve()` now returns `renderSystemPrompt(user?)`, the catalog carries it, and `resolveEffectiveContext` renders before hashing — so a snapshot stays addressed by what was actually sent, two owners on one model bind distinct snapshots, and a profile edit mints a new one rather than mutating the old.\n\nBoot still renders once with the model context alone, keeping the existing `rendered prompt is empty` failure: that probe is the minimum possible output, so a template non-empty there is non-empty for every owner, and a prompt wrapped entirely in `{{#if user}}` correctly fails startup.\n\nBlast radius stayed small because everything downstream reads the persisted snapshot, not the catalog — `run-execution` and `compaction` both take `snapshot.systemPrompt`, and titling has its own prompt, so per-user context never touches the title path.\n\n## Fence integrity\n\nOwner text lands inside a named `<user_personalization>` block, preceded by framing that tells the model the contents are data describing the user, rank below the system instructions and the current conversation, and cannot grant tools or override safety rules.\n\nThe fence's guarantee is a **tag-balance invariant, not blanket escaping**. Blanket `&<>` escaping entity-mangled legitimately tag-structured preference text — owners write `<instructions>…</instructions>` blocks, and mangling every angle bracket destroys exactly the structure that text exists to convey. Two rules replace it, for authored fields only:\n\n1. **A value can never close a tag it did not open within that same value.** Matching is positional (a counter would pass `</x>text<x>`) and recovers HTML-style past phantom openers left by prose mentions like \"follow `<answering_rules>`\". Unmatched, malformed, or whitespace-padded closers are escaped fail-closed.\n2. **The fence's own name is reserved** and never emitted as a tag, opener or closer, matched or not.\n\nRule 2 exists because review caught that rule 1 alone accepts a value that both opens *and* closes `<user_personalization>` — satisfying the stated invariant while rendering a complete forged fence inside the real one (reproduced against the real pipeline: 2 openers, 2 closers). Account-identity values, short and markup-free, keep the strict `&`/`<`/`>` escape.\n\nDeliberately **not** defended, and documented as such: authored text can spell a `###` heading of its own. Stripping markdown would mangle text owners legitimately write, and both authored fields carry identical authority — a forged heading relabels the owner's own text inside the owner's own data block.\n\nThe authority bound is enforced by structure, not wording: `resolveAdvertisedTools` receives no personalization input at all, and a test proves the advertised tool contract is byte-identical with and without a profile that explicitly demands a non-allowlisted tool.\n\n## Absence, and why it is omission\n\nAbsence is expressed by **omission at three levels** — a field with no value, then `user.personalization` when nothing authored survives, then `user` itself when nothing beneath it would render. That is what lets one `{{#if user}}` gate a whole section including its framing prose, and what keeps content-addressed snapshots deduping for unpersonalized owners. Omission is required rather than stylistic: escaped values are marked already-safe, and such a wrapper is truthy even when it wraps an empty string.\n\nImplementation surfaced a gap the spec had missed — `{{#if user}}` needs `user` as a path, but the allowlist held only leaf values, so the packaged prompt failed its own boot validation. Rather than widen the value allowlist (which would let `{{user}}` render a stringified object), validation now splits by **position**: `user` and `user.personalization` are legal as a conditional's subject and still rejected as output.\n\n## Compaction\n\nThe summarization call replays the bound system prompt — including the rendered block — and asks for constraints and preferences, so a standing profile could be copied into a `conversation-checkpoint` that is persisted, replayed forever, and unreachable by any later edit. Both instruction constants (the full-current and the transition path, which would otherwise have been missed) now tell the model not to carry content out of `<user_personalization>`, naming the delimiter rather than asking it to work out where a preference originated.\n\nIt costs no cache: the instruction is the request's final user message, already outside the byte-identical prefix, whereas stripping the block from the replayed prompt would make the whole deliberately-large call cold.\n\n## Storage and API\n\nA tenant-owned table keyed on `user_id` with RLS `ENABLE`d **and** `FORCE`d and **no public-read branch** — unlike chats, personalization must stay unreachable through the shared-chat path. The `UPDATE` policy carries `WITH CHECK` as well as `USING`, so a caller cannot rewrite `user_id` to hand their row to someone else.\n\nProven against a real Postgres as the owning role: cross-tenant read, targeted read by owner id, the empty (public) identity, cross-tenant `UPDATE` and `DELETE`, forging a row for another user, and the row-handoff attempt. The suite also pins the gap it cannot close — `users` carries no RLS, so the account read feeding `user.name`/`user.email` has no datastore backstop, and the test asserts an unfiltered read really does return every account, which is why that query's owner filter is explicit and documented where it lives.\n\n`GET`/`PATCH /api/v1/me/personalization` expose it, with caps of 255/8000/8000 enforced at the DTO. `PATCH` distinguishes an omitted key (keep) from an explicit `null` (clear), so a single-field update cannot wipe the rest of a profile.\n\n## Settings UI\n\nA personalization section at `/settings`: master switch sitting directly on the three fields it gates, then Save, then the account-identity switch, then a preview mirror.\n\nThe preview reproduces the server's rendering **byte-for-byte**, including whitespace — a preview captioned \"exactly as it is sent\" is worth nothing if it normalizes spacing the server does not. Both sides are pinned to identical literals so the next drift fails a test. It duplicates the sanitizer rather than approximating it, with a parity suite against the api's cases.\n\n## Review findings addressed\n\nA high-effort multi-agent review of the sanitizer commit produced nine verified findings; seven were fixed in `3b4814fd`:\n\n- the balanced-fence forgery and the whitespace-padded-closer bypass described above\n- absent fields left stray blank lines in the rendered fence (conditionals were not Handlebars-standalone), and the preview reproduced none of them\n- section spacing diverged between preview and server whenever both multi-line fields were set\n- duplicated trim/omit helpers folded into one transform-parameterized function per side\n- the web parity fixture aligned with the api suite it claims to mirror\n\nTwo skipped: the markdown-heading limitation above (documented in the spec instead), and Storybook play-function coverage for the preview DOM — the component has no story at all and would need three `__mocks__` modules plus preview registration, which is separate work.\n\n## Deliberately not shipped\n\nPer-model activation reporting, a rendered-token estimate, and a `timezone` field — a timezone is a zone rather than an instant, and nothing injects the current time, so it could not have resolved \"tomorrow\" anyway; it waits for the change that adds current-time injection.\n\nNote that an operator prompt referencing no per-user path silently forgoes personalization for that model. Replacing `chat-default.md` therefore makes an owner's toggles inert there, with nothing reporting it. Documented, accepted.\n\n## Spec\n\n`add-user-personalization` is archived under `openspec/changes/archive/2026-08-04-add-user-personalization/`, with its deltas synced into `openspec/specs/personalization` (new capability), `instance-config`, and `model-system-prompts`. `SPEC.md` §20.1 indexes the capability. `openspec validate --all --strict` passes 17/17.\n\nTwo pre-existing instance-config scenarios asserted escaping \"alters exactly three characters\" for any rendered value; the two-regime split makes that false for authored fields, so both were scoped to model and identity values rather than left contradicting the requirement above them.\n\n## Verification\n\napi 553 unit tests, web 318 tests, typecheck/lint/format clean on both, `openspec validate --all --strict` 17/17. Integration and browser E2E are CI's call — this box's known local environment issues make them unreliable here.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_014BCPobQSCvqFqbgHk2aZzo\n",
