            "content": "**Layer 1 of 5** — design only, no code. Docs and OpenSpec artifacts.\n\n---\n\n## What the whole stack delivers\n\nOwner-authored personalization: a `preferredName`, an `about`, and `responsePreferences`, stored per user and rendered into that owner's system prompt on every run. Two toggles gate it, and their defaults are deliberately asymmetric:\n\n- `enabled` defaults **on**, because it gates only what the owner typed — someone who wrote nothing renders nothing, and their text works the moment they write it, with no second switch to discover.\n- `shareAccountIdentity` defaults **off**, because it gates account-derived identity, and defaulting it on would move every existing user's address to the operator's configured provider retroactively, with no action or awareness from those users.\n\nIt builds on the Handlebars prompt-templating work that merged earlier.\n\n## Why five layers\n\nThe layers have genuinely different review concerns and risk profiles, which is the actual reason for splitting — not raw line count. Whoever should scrutinize `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` policies is not who should check a settings-page scroll container.\n\n| Layer | Concern | Reviewer looking for |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 (this) | Design decisions | Is the shape right before reading code |\n| 2 | Storage, RLS, render-seam inversion | Tenant isolation; a structural change to when prompts render |\n| 3 | HTTP surface, compaction | API contract; a standing profile leaking into a frozen checkpoint |\n| 4 | Settings UI | Layout, a11y, mutation discipline |\n| 5 | Prompt-injection sanitizer, spec sync | Fence integrity — the security-critical layer |\n\nEvery layer boundary was verified to build and pass tests on its own, so no layer lands with red CI. Layers split at existing commit boundaries with no rewriting: the branch history was already in dependency order.\n\n## This layer\n\nThe OpenSpec planning record for `add-user-personalization` — proposal, design, delta specs, tasks — plus the background research on cross-lingual recall and user-context injection that motivated it.\n\nThe commits are the decision trail, and several reverse earlier positions on purpose:\n\n- `${...}` expression names moved to `user.`-namespaced paths, then to Handlebars once that prerequisite landed.\n- D4a first excluded the account email, then `db5f6d1` reversed it — the operator, not llame, decides whether an address reaches the provider, and withholding it made `shareAccountIdentity` meaningless.\n- The `enabled` default was settled as two asymmetric switches rather than one, for the reason above.\n- Handlebars behavior was pinned from empirical probes rather than assumed — `SafeString` is truthy even when wrapping `\"\"`, and `Handlebars.create()` shares `Utils` by reference with the global export.\n\nNo application code is touched, so there is nothing here that can break at runtime.\n",
