            "content": "**Layer 3 of 5** — the owner-facing HTTP surface, and stopping personalization from being frozen into a checkpoint. Stacked on #271.\n\n## API\n\n`GET` / `PATCH /api/v1/me/personalization`, establishing `api/v1/me` as the namespace for resources belonging to the authenticated user.\n\nIdentity comes only from the authenticated session and never from client-supplied input, so a caller cannot read or write another user's personalization by supplying an identifier. Caps of 255 / 8000 / 8000 are enforced at the DTO rather than in the schema, so changing one is not a migration.\n\n`PATCH` distinguishes an **omitted key** (keep the stored value) from an **explicit `null`** (clear it), so a single-field update cannot silently wipe the rest of a profile.\n\n`GET` never 404s: an owner who has written nothing gets the defaults back, so there is no first-use branch for clients to handle.\n\n## Compaction exclusion\n\nThe summarization call replays the bound system prompt — including the rendered personalization block — and asks the model for constraints and preferences. Without a guard, a standing profile gets copied into a `conversation-checkpoint` that is persisted, replayed forever, and unreachable by any later edit to the profile. Clearing a field would not clear it from history.\n\nBoth instruction constants now tell the model not to carry content out of `<user_personalization>`: the full-current path **and** the transition path, which would otherwise have been missed. The instruction names the delimiter rather than asking the model to work out where a preference originated — a syntactic instruction being considerably more reliable than a provenance judgment.\n\n**It costs no prompt cache.** The instruction is the request's final user message, already outside the byte-identical cached prefix. Stripping the block from the replayed system prompt instead would invalidate the prefix and make the whole deliberately-large summarization call cold — precisely at the point in the context window where it is most expensive.\n\n## Authority bound\n\nResponse preferences rank below the operator prompt and cannot grant capabilities. That bound is enforced **structurally, not by wording**: `resolveAdvertisedTools` receives no personalization input at all, and a test proves the advertised tool contract is byte-identical with and without a profile that explicitly demands a non-allowlisted tool.\n\nEvery rung below the top one is advisory — carried by the packaged default's framing prose and model compliance — and the spec says so rather than implying more enforcement than exists.\n\n## Also here\n\n`f580b5c4` is a cleanup sweep over the preceding commits (reuse, simplification, efficiency, altitude), including replacing `as unknown as` casts with `Pick<Class, 'method'>` + `@Inject(Class)` — which removed two pre-existing casts as a side effect. The pattern is banned in this repo; #268 tracks documenting and linting it.\n\n## Verification\n\napi typecheck, lint, 536 unit tests; web typecheck and 297 tests green at this commit.\n",
