            "content": "**Layer 5 of 5** — the security-critical layer. Stacked on #273. Read this one adversarially.\n\n## The problem with blanket escaping\n\nEarlier layers escaped `&`, `<`, `>` in every rendered value. That kept the `<user_personalization>` fence intact, but it entity-mangled legitimately tag-structured preference text: owners write `<instructions>…</instructions>` blocks, and converting every angle bracket destroys exactly the structure that text exists to convey. The model copes with `&lt;instructions&gt;` but loses the structural signal the tags are there for.\n\n## What replaces it\n\nFor authored fields only — a tag sanitizer with two rules:\n\n**1. A value can never close a tag it did not open within that same value.**\nMatching is positional, not a counter: `</x>text<x>` is count-balanced yet its closer precedes its opener, so it would close the *enclosing* tag. It recovers HTML-style past phantom openers, because prose like `- ALWAYS follow <answering_rules>` reads as an opening tag, and strict innermost-matching would then escape the legitimate outer `</instructions>`. Unmatched, malformed, or whitespace-padded closers are escaped fail-closed regardless of stack state — a model may honor a spelling a strict parser rejects.\n\n**2. The fence's own name is reserved** and never emitted as a tag, opener or closer, matched or not.\n\nRule 2 exists because **review caught that rule 1 alone is insufficient.** A value that both opens *and* closes `<user_personalization>` satisfies the stated invariant — it closes only what it opened — while rendering a complete forged fence inside the real one. Reproduced against the real pipeline: two openers, two closers.\n\nEverything else passes byte-for-byte. `R&D` and `a < b` stop being mangled as a side effect. Account-identity values, short and markup-free, keep the strict escape.\n\nThe sanitizer is mirrored in `apps/web` for the preview, with a parity suite over the same fixtures so a behavioral drift