            "content": "> **Layer 1 of 8** — the OpenSpec design record for personalization. Docs only; no application code.\n\n## What the stack delivers\n\nOwner-authored personalization: a `preferredName`, an `about`, and `responsePreferences`, stored per user and rendered into that owner's system prompt on every run. Two toggles gate it, with deliberately asymmetric defaults:\n\n- `enabled` defaults **on** — it gates only what the owner typed, so someone who wrote nothing renders nothing, and their text works the moment they write it.\n- `shareAccountIdentity` defaults **off** — it gates account-derived identity, and defaulting it on would move every existing user's address to the operator's configured provider retroactively, with no action or awareness from those users.\n\nBuilt on the Handlebars prompt-templating prerequisite that merged earlier.\n\n## The layers\n\nEach is one concern with a distinguishable reviewer audience:\n\n| | Layer | In one sentence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `spec` | The design record — this PR. |\n| 2 | `schema` | The personalization table, with owner-only FORCE RLS. |\n| 3 | `render-seam` | Render each model's prompt per run instead of at boot. |\n| 4 | `api` | Owner-scoped read and update of your own personalization. |\n| 5 | `compaction` | Keep personalization out of compaction checkpoints. |\n| 6 | `web` | The settings section. |\n| 7 | `sanitizer` | Replace blanket escaping with a tag-balance sanitizer. |\n| 8 | `spec-sync` | Sync the deltas into the main specs and archive the change. |\n\nEvery boundary was verified to typecheck and pass tests on its own, so no layer lands with red CI. The layers split at existing commit boundaries — nothing was rebased or cherry-picked, and the top of the stack is byte-identical to the branch it came from.\n\n## This layer\n\nThe planning record: proposal, design, delta specs, tasks, plus the background research on cross-lingual recall and user-context injection that motivated it.\n\nThe commits are the decision trail, and several reverse earlier positions on purpose:\n\n- Expression names moved from `${...}` to `user.`-namespaced paths, then to Handlebars once that prerequisite landed.\n- D4a first excluded the account email; `db5f6d1` reversed it — the operator, not llame, decides whether an address reaches the provider, and withholding it made `shareAccountIdentity` meaningless.\n- The `enabled` default was settled as two asymmetric switches rather than one.\n- Handlebars behavior was pinned from empirical probes rather than assumed: `SafeString` is truthy even when wrapping `\"\"`, and `Handlebars.create()` shares `Utils` **by reference** with the global export, so patching `escapeExpression` would change escaping for every consumer in the process.\n",
