            "content": "> **Layer 2 of 8** — the personalization table, with owner-only FORCE RLS and no public-read branch.\n\n> **On the size:** 2536 of the ~2946 added lines are one Drizzle-generated migration snapshot (`meta/20260803201518_snapshot.json`), machine-written and unreviewable by design. Hand-written code here is roughly 400 lines.\n\n## Shape\n\nA tenant-owned table keyed on `user_id` — the user id **is** the primary key, not a surrogate, because an owner has exactly one profile.\n\n## Isolation\n\nRLS is `ENABLE`d **and** `FORCE`d. Drizzle's `.enableRLS()` emits only `ENABLE`, so the `FORCE` statement is hand-appended to the migration. Without it the owning `app` role bypasses all four owner policies — which in a single-role self-hosted deployment means no isolation at all on a table holding owner-authored profile text.\n\nThere is **no public-read branch**. Unlike chats, personalization must stay unreachable through the shared-chat path even for an owner who has published a chat.\n\nThe `UPDATE` policy carries `WITH CHECK` as well as `USING`. `USING` selects which rows may be updated; `WITH CHECK` constrains the result — without it a caller could rewrite `user_id` and hand their row to someone else.\n\n## Proof\n\nExercised against a real Postgres as the owning role: cross-tenant read, targeted read by owner id, the empty (public) identity, cross-tenant `UPDATE` and `DELETE`, forging a row for another user, and the row-handoff attempt.\n\nThe suite also pins the gap it **cannot** close. `users` carries no RLS, so the account read that will later feed `user.name`/`user.email` has no datastore backstop. The test asserts that an unfiltered read really does return every account — which is why that query's owner filter is explicit and documented where it lives, rather than trusted to the datastore.\n\nCaps live at the DTO rather than in the schema (layer 4), so changing one is not a migration.\n\n## Verification\n\napi typecheck, lint, and 511 unit tests green at this commit. The RLS proof runs in the integration suite against a throwaway Postgres provisioned with the same non-superuser owner + FORCE topology.\n",
