            "content": "> **Layer 5 of 8** — keep a standing personalization profile from being frozen into a compaction checkpoint.\n\n## The problem\n\nThe summarization call replays the bound system prompt — including the rendered personalization block — and asks the model for constraints and preferences. Without a guard, a standing profile gets copied into a `conversation-checkpoint` that is persisted, replayed forever, and unreachable by any later edit. Clearing a field would not clear it from history.\n\n## The fix, and where it goes\n\nBoth instruction constants now tell the model not to carry content out of `<user_personalization>`: the full-current path **and** the transition path, which would otherwise have been missed.\n\nThe instruction names the delimiter rather than asking the model to work out where a preference originated. A syntactic instruction is considerably more reliable than a provenance judgment.\n\n**It costs no prompt cache.** The instruction is the request's final user message, already outside the byte-identical cached prefix. Stripping the block from the replayed system prompt instead would invalidate that prefix and make the whole deliberately-large summarization call cold — precisely at the point in the context window where it is most expensive. This was the reviewed alternative and it was rejected for that reason.\n\n## Authority bound\n\nResponse preferences rank below the operator prompt and cannot grant capabilities. That bound is enforced **structurally, not by wording**: `resolveAdvertisedTools` receives no personalization input at all, and a test proves the advertised tool contract is byte-identical with and without a profile that explicitly demands a non-allowlisted tool.\n\nEvery rung below the top one is advisory — carried by the packaged default's framing prose and model compliance — and the spec says so rather than implying more enforcement than exists.\n\n## Also here\n\n`f580b5c4` is a cleanup sweep over the preceding api layers (reuse, simplification, efficiency, altitude). It replaces `as unknown as` casts with `Pick<Class, 'method'>` + `@Inject(Class)`, which removed two pre-existing casts as a side effect. The pattern is banned in this repo; #268 tracks documenting and linting it.\n\n## Verification\n\napi typecheck, lint, 536 unit tests; web typecheck and 297 tests green at this commit.\n",
