            "content": "> **Layer 7 of 8** — replace blanket escaping with a tag-balance sanitizer, so authored markup survives without being able to forge the fence.\n\nThe security-critical layer. Read this one adversarially.\n\n## Why blanket escaping had to go\n\nEarlier layers escaped `&`, `<`, `>` in every rendered value. That kept the `<user_personalization>` fence intact, but it entity-mangled legitimately tag-structured preference text: owners write `<instructions>…</instructions>` blocks, and converting every angle bracket destroys exactly the structure that text exists to convey.\n\n## Two rules replace it, for authored fields only\n\n**1. A value can never close a tag it did not open within that same value.**\n\nMatching is positional, not a counter — `</x>text<x>` is count-balanced yet its closer precedes its opener, so it would close the *enclosing* tag. It recovers HTML-style past phantom openers, because prose like `- ALWAYS follow <answering_rules>` reads as an opening tag, and strict innermost-matching would then escape the legitimate outer `</instructions>`. Unmatched, malformed, or whitespace-padded closers are escaped fail-closed regardless of stack state — a model may honor a spelling a strict parser rejects.\n\n**2. The fence's own name is reserved**, never emitted as a tag, opener or closer, matched or not.\n\nRule 2 exists because **review caught that rule 1 alone is insufficient**. A value that both opens *and* closes `<user_personalization>` satisfies the stated invariant — it closes only what it opened — while rendering a complete forged fence inside the real one. Reproduced against the real pipeline: two openers, two closers.\n\nEverything else passes byte-for-byte; `R&D` and `a < b` stop being mangled as a side effect. Account-identity values, short and markup-free, keep the strict escape. The sanitizer is mirrored in `apps/web` for the preview, with a parity suite over the same fixtures so a drift fails on whichever side changed.\n\n## Block layout\n\nMulti-line fields render as `###` subsections rather than `Label: value` lines — a colon label reads wrongly once the value spans paragraphs. Single-line entries are grouped above them so none appears to belong to a heading.\n\nEvery conditional is Handlebars-standalone, so an absent field leaves no residual blank line. Before this, an owner with only `about` set got four stray blank lines inside the fence and the preview reproduced none of them — a preview captioned \"exactly as it is sent\" is worth nothing if it normalizes whitespace the server does not. Both sides are now pinned to identical byte-exact literals.\n\n## Not defended, and documented as such\n\nAuthored text can spell a `###` heading of its own. Stripping markdown would mangle text owners legitimately write, and both authored fields carry identical authority — a forged heading relabels the owner's own text inside the owner's own data block and confers nothing. Only the delimiter is structurally guaranteed. An operator whose replacement template wraps per-user content in a differently-named tag keeps rule 1 but not rule 2, which the spec states rather than implies away.\n\n## Review\n\nA high-effort multi-agent review of the first sanitizer commit produced nine verified findings; seven are fixed here — the forged fence and padded-closer bypass above, the blank-line divergence, the section-spacing divergence, two duplicated trim/omit helpers folded into one transform-parameterized function per side, and a parity fixture that did not match the suite it claimed to mirror. Two were skipped with reasons stated: the markdown-heading limitation, and Storybook coverage for the preview DOM.\n\n## Verification\n\napi typecheck, lint, 553 unit tests; web typecheck, lint, 318 tests. The real stored profile that motivated the change renders with zero entities introduced and exactly one fence closer.\n",
